1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:21,240
The school supply framework has three anchors: diastolic time,
coronary patency and arterial oxygen content. Left ventricular
coronary perfusion occurs predominantly during diastole, when
compression of vessels by contracting muscle is reduced. This is why a
faster rate can reduce the time available for supply.

2
00:00:21,240 --> 00:00:41,328
An open vessel is not sufficient if perfusion pressure is poor or
oxygen content is inadequate. Oxygen content depends heavily on
haemoglobin and saturation. A normal saturation does not establish a
normal haemoglobin concentration or guarantee adequate myocardial
oxygen delivery.

3
00:00:41,328 --> 00:01:00,240
Demand reflects how much work the myocardium performs. A faster heart
rate means more contractions per minute; stronger contractility and
higher afterload can increase work and oxygen use. Interpret these
factors together instead of assuming one rate predicts oxygen demand
for everyone.

4
00:01:00,240 --> 00:01:18,912
The supply and demand questions complement rhythm naming. Ask whether
available coronary flow and oxygen content can meet the work required
of this heart now. A patient with coronary disease, anaemia or
impaired pumping may tolerate a rhythm poorly even when another
patient tolerates it well.

5
00:01:18,912 --> 00:01:36,216
Sustained tachycardia can simultaneously raise demand and shorten
diastolic supply time. It can also impair ventricular filling,
reducing stroke volume. This explains why a fast rhythm may become
dangerous before the monitor changes to a different named rhythm.

6
00:01:36,216 --> 00:01:56,040
However, tachycardia may be compensating for blood loss, fever or
another illness. The mechanism guides the response: address the
patient’s condition through the authorized pathway rather than
reflexively reducing every fast rate. Determine whether the rhythm is
cause, consequence or both.

7
00:01:56,040 --> 00:02:15,000
A slow rate can cause a different problem: even with low demand,
cardiac output may be insufficient if stroke volume cannot compensate.
Low output can reduce arterial and coronary perfusion pressure. More
time between beats is not automatically protective if not enough blood
is being delivered.

8
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:34,776
Assess mentation, pulse, skin, pressure trend and symptoms to decide
whether the patient is tolerating the rate. Do not use either a
bradycardia cutoff or a normal-looking electrical pattern as the
entire decision. Important block can deteriorate despite a temporarily
adequate pressure reading.

9
00:02:34,776 --> 00:02:54,912
Coordinated atrial contraction contributes to late ventricular
filling. Losing that coordination in AF or AV dissociation can reduce
filling and stroke volume, especially in older or stiff ventricles.
The importance is patient-dependent; it is not a fixed percentage to
apply to every heart.

10
00:02:54,912 --> 00:03:15,144
Ventricular pacing may restore rate without restoring physiological AV
timing. Therefore a paced rate that appears normal still requires
assessment of pulse and perfusion. Evaluate the complete supply–demand
balance rather than equating a device-generated number with normal
cardiovascular function.

11
00:03:15,144 --> 00:03:35,136
A prolonged QT chiefly identifies a problem of repolarization
vulnerability rather than simply excessive myocardial work. Medication
exposure, electrolytes and inherited susceptibility matter. A
comfortable patient with normal pressure can still have a concerning
repolarization finding.

12
00:03:35,136 --> 00:03:53,832
Use both frameworks. Ask whether oxygen delivery meets current work,
and independently ask whether recovery is electrically vulnerable.
State the mechanism and the evidence supporting it, then choose
monitoring, communication and escalation consistent with the patient’s
condition and your authority.
